window.ENGRD2700={"skills":[{"id":"data.graphical_summary","name":"Histograms, boxplots & distribution shape","unit":"Data","priority":"medium","cue":"You are given raw data or a plot and must describe center, spread, skew, or unusual values.","intuition":"A graph is a compressed story of the data. First ask: where is the mass, how wide is it, which side has the long tail, and are any points unusually far away?","model":"Describe shape first, then center, then spread, then unusual values. Right-skew means the tail points right; left-skew means the tail points left.","why":"This is the same kind of first-pass diagnosis you would do on product metrics, tennis serve speeds, or bug-resolution times before choosing a model.","trap":"Do not call a distribution right-skewed because most observations are on the right. Skew is named for the direction of the tail.","transfer":"If a startup has many ordinary purchase amounts and a few huge enterprise purchases, picture the histogram before calculating anything."},{"id":"data.center_quantiles","name":"Mean, median & quantiles","unit":"Data","priority":"high","cue":"You are asked for a typical value, ordered position, percentile, or quantile.","intuition":"The mean balances all values; the median cuts the ordered data in half; a quantile marks a chosen cumulative position.","model":"Sort first for medians and quantiles. Mean uses every value. A p-quantile is a value with about proportion p at or below it.","why":"Mean and median answer different product questions: average revenue can be pulled up by whales, while median user behavior shows the typical person.","trap":"Never take a median or quantile from unsorted data. Also remember that an extreme value can move the mean a lot while barely changing the median.","transfer":"Think about NYC apartment rents: one penthouse can change the mean much more than the median."},{"id":"data.variance_sd","name":"Variance & standard deviation","unit":"Data","priority":"high","cue":"You are asked to measure spread or compare variability.","intuition":"Spread asks how far observations typically wander from the center. Variance squares those distances; standard deviation converts the result back to the original units.","model":"Variance averages squared distance from the mean. Standard deviation is its square root and returns to the original units.","why":"Two tennis players can have the same average serve speed but very different consistency. Standard deviation captures that consistency.","trap":"Variance is in squared units; standard deviation is in the original units. Do not compare a variance directly to a mean as if they had the same units.","transfer":"When two systems have the same average response time, the one with smaller standard deviation is more predictable."},{"id":"data.standardization","name":"Standardization / z-scores","unit":"Data","priority":"high","cue":"You need to express a value relative to a mean in standard-deviation units.","intuition":"A z-score replaces the original units with a universal ruler: how many standard deviations above or below the mean?","model":"z=(x-mean)/sd. Positive is above the mean; negative is below. Magnitude tells how many standard deviations away.","why":"It lets you compare unlike scales, such as an exam score and a tennis statistic, by asking how exceptional each is relative to its own group.","trap":"Subtract the mean before dividing by the standard deviation. A positive z-score only means above that distribution's mean, not automatically 'good.'","transfer":"A 90 on one exam and an 80 on another can be compared fairly only after accounting for each exam's center and spread."},{"id":"count.perm_comb","name":"Permutations vs combinations","unit":"Counting","priority":"high","cue":"Count selections: ask whether order matters and whether replacement occurs.","intuition":"Before touching a formula, ask whether swapping two chosen items creates a genuinely different outcome.","model":"Order matters -> permutations. Order does not matter -> combinations. Then check replacement and whether identical groups cause overcounting.","why":"This distinction appears in team selection, ranking candidates, assigning presentation slots, and arranging product experiments.","trap":"Do not choose a formula by pattern matching numbers. Decide what one outcome actually is, then ask whether order changes that outcome.","transfer":"Choosing 3 people for a team is different from choosing a president, vice president, and treasurer from the same people."},{"id":"count.partitions","name":"Groups, partitions & overcounting","unit":"Counting","priority":"high","cue":"Items are divided into unlabeled groups; naive combinations count equivalent group labels more than once.","intuition":"You often build groups correctly and then accidentally count the same final grouping several times because you secretly labeled the groups.","model":"Build the groups, then divide by the number of labelings that represent the same partition, often a factorial.","why":"This matters when splitting classmates into project teams or attendees into identical discussion groups where 'Group 1' and 'Group 2' are not meaningful labels.","trap":"If the groups are unlabeled and equal-sized, swapping whole groups does not create a new partition.","transfer":"If six friends are split into two unlabeled groups of three, choosing one group automatically determines the other."},{"id":"prob.events","name":"Events, complements, unions & intersections","unit":"Probability","priority":"high","cue":"Translate words such as and/or/not/at least/at most into event operations.","intuition":"Probability language is set language. 'And' narrows to overlap; 'or' combines; 'not' flips to the complement.","model":"AND -> intersection. OR -> union. NOT -> complement. 'At least one' is often easiest as 1-P(none).","why":"Many real decisions fail because the verbal event was translated incorrectly before any arithmetic began.","trap":"In probability, 'A or B' normally includes the possibility that both occur unless the problem explicitly says 'but not both.'","transfer":"For two software checks, 'at least one fails' is often easier to compute as one minus 'both pass.'"},{"id":"prob.inclusion_exclusion","name":"Inclusion-exclusion","unit":"Probability","priority":"high","cue":"You know overlapping event probabilities and need a union or intersection.","intuition":"When you add P(A) and P(B), the overlap gets counted twice, so subtract it once.","model":"P(A union B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A intersection B). The subtraction fixes double-counting.","why":"It is the probability version of deduplicating users who appear in two product segments.","trap":"Do not subtract the overlap when finding an intersection. Inclusion-exclusion is mainly a correction for union double-counting.","transfer":"If some users are both iPhone and iPad users, adding the two user counts overstates total unique Apple-device users."},{"id":"prob.equally_likely","name":"Equally likely outcomes","unit":"Probability","priority":"medium","cue":"A finite sample space has equally likely outcomes, so probability is favorable divided by total.","intuition":"Counting gives probability only when every elementary outcome you count has the same chance.","model":"When every elementary outcome is equally likely, count favorable outcomes and divide by the total number of outcomes.","why":"This is the bridge from combinatorics to probability in cards, dice, random team selection, and shuffled choices.","trap":"Do not use favorable/total if your listed outcomes are not equally likely. 'Heads or tails' is fine for a fair coin; 'rain or no rain' is not automatically 1/2.","transfer":"If a random student is chosen uniformly from a roster, each student is equally likely; if chosen by volunteering, they are not."},{"id":"cond.conditional","name":"Conditional probability","unit":"Conditional probability","priority":"high","cue":"New information restricts the sample space: given B, what is the chance of A?","intuition":"Conditioning means you throw away worlds inconsistent with what you just learned and renormalize inside what remains.","model":"P(A|B)=P(A intersection B)/P(B). The denominator is the world that remains after learning B.","why":"This is how you update risk after seeing evidence: a user clicked, a bug report has logs, or a serve landed in.","trap":"P(A|B) and P(B|A) are different questions. Read the word after 'given' as the new denominator world.","transfer":"Among bug reports that include reproduction steps, what fraction also include environment details?"},{"id":"cond.total_bayes","name":"Total probability & Bayes","unit":"Conditional probability","priority":"high","cue":"You observe evidence and reason backward to which source or cause produced it.","intuition":"Bayes asks: after seeing evidence, which hidden cause is now plausible? Start with prior prevalence, weight by how likely the evidence is under each cause, then normalize.","model":"Posterior = likelihood × prior / total evidence probability. Total probability builds the denominator by adding all mutually exclusive ways the evidence could occur.","why":"This is exactly the logic behind interpreting screening tests, anomaly alerts, classifier outputs, and product signals.","trap":"A highly accurate test can still have a low positive predictive value when the underlying condition is extremely rare.","transfer":"If only a tiny share of bug reports contain a real regression, even a good detector can generate many false alarms."},{"id":"cond.independence","name":"Independence","unit":"Conditional probability","priority":"high","cue":"Ask whether learning one event changes the probability of another.","intuition":"Independent means information about one event gives you no update about the other.","model":"Independent means P(A intersection B)=P(A)P(B), equivalently P(A|B)=P(A) when defined.","why":"Independence is the assumption that makes repeated-trial models such as the binomial work.","trap":"Mutually exclusive events are usually not independent. If one happens and prevents the other, learning one changes the probability of the other.","transfer":"Two coin flips can be independent; drawing two cards without replacement is not."},{"id":"rv.discrete_pmf_cdf","name":"Discrete PMF <-> CDF","unit":"Random variables","priority":"high","cue":"A discrete variable is given by point probabilities or a step CDF.","intuition":"The PMF stores probability at each exact value. The CDF is a running total. Every jump in the CDF is one PMF mass.","model":"PMF gives jump sizes. CDF accumulates probability up to x. For a discrete CDF, each jump equals P(X=x).","why":"Think of issue counts or number of successful serves: exact counts are masses, while the CDF answers 'at most this many.'","trap":"For a discrete variable, F(x)=P(X<=x), including the mass at x. Left limits matter when extracting a jump.","transfer":"If the CDF rises from .55 to .80 at x=3, then exactly 25% of the probability sits at X=3."},{"id":"rv.expectation","name":"Expectation & E[g(X)]","unit":"Random variables","priority":"high","cue":"Compute long-run average payoff or E[g(X)] from a PMF/PDF.","intuition":"Expectation is a probability-weighted average across possible futures, not necessarily a value you will ever literally observe.","model":"Weight each possible transformed value g(x) by its probability or density, then sum or integrate.","why":"Expected value is the core language of decisions under uncertainty: pricing, experiments, investments, queues, and strategy.","trap":"E[g(X)] is generally not g(E[X]). Apply the transformation to each possible value before averaging unless a valid rule says otherwise.","transfer":"A product feature can have positive expected impact even if most individual users see no change, because rare large gains can matter."},{"id":"rv.variance_linear","name":"Variance & linear transformations","unit":"Random variables","priority":"high","cue":"Find spread, or transform Y=aX+b and update mean/variance.","intuition":"Adding a constant shifts everyone together, so spread does not change. Multiplying stretches distances from the mean, so variance scales by the square.","model":"E[aX+b]=aE[X]+b. Var(aX+b)=a^2 Var(X). Adding a constant shifts center but not spread.","why":"This lets you transform units or rescale scores without rebuilding the distribution from scratch.","trap":"The +b disappears from variance, and the multiplier is squared: Var(aX)=a^2 Var(X), not a Var(X).","transfer":"Converting a time measure from minutes to seconds multiplies standard deviation by 60 and variance by 3600."},{"id":"dist.binomial","name":"Binomial model","unit":"Discrete distributions","priority":"high","cue":"Fixed n independent trials, same p, count successes.","intuition":"A binomial variable counts how many successes happen across a fixed number of yes/no trials under stable conditions.","model":"X~Bin(n,p). P(X=k)=C(n,k)p^k(1-p)^(n-k). Mean=np, variance=np(1-p).","why":"This maps naturally to serves in, conversions, pass/fail checks, and repeated experiment outcomes.","trap":"Check all four conditions: fixed n, two outcomes per trial, constant p, and independence. Without replacement often breaks independence.","transfer":"If you take 20 independent serves with the same in-probability, the number that land in is binomial."},{"id":"dist.poisson","name":"Poisson & approximation","unit":"Discrete distributions","priority":"high","cue":"Count events in an interval, or approximate Binomial when n is large and p is small.","intuition":"Poisson counts scattered arrivals over a fixed exposure: bugs per day, messages per hour, failures per mile.","model":"X~Pois(lambda), mean=variance=lambda. For Bin(n,p), Poisson lambda=np is useful when p is small.","why":"It is a natural model for operational event counts and a convenient approximation for many rare independent opportunities.","trap":"Lambda is the expected count for the interval you are modeling. If the interval doubles, lambda doubles.","transfer":"If an open-source repo averages 3 qualifying bug reports per day, a two-day count would use lambda=6."},{"id":"dist.geometric","name":"Geometric model","unit":"Discrete distributions","priority":"high","cue":"Number of independent trials until the first success.","intuition":"Geometric asks how long you keep trying until the first success. To finish on trial k, you must fail k-1 times and then succeed.","model":"P(X=k)=(1-p)^(k-1)p for k=1,2,... It is memoryless.","why":"It models attempts-until-success situations like first conversion, first ace, or first successful test run.","trap":"Know which convention your course uses. Here X counts the successful trial itself, so X starts at 1.","transfer":"If each independent pitch has a 20% chance of getting a yes, the number of pitches until the first yes is geometric."},{"id":"dist.hypergeometric","name":"Hypergeometric model","unit":"Discrete distributions","priority":"medium","cue":"Sample without replacement from a finite population with two types.","intuition":"Hypergeometric is the without-replacement cousin of binomial: once you draw one item, the composition of what remains changes.","model":"P(X=k)=C(K,k)C(N-K,n-k)/C(N,n) when drawing n items without replacement from N with K successes.","why":"It models audits, sampling repositories, selecting people, or drawing physical items when the same item cannot be selected twice.","trap":"If you sample without replacement from a small finite population, do not pretend trials are independent binomial trials.","transfer":"Sampling 5 bug reports from a fixed batch of 20 without replacement is hypergeometric if 7 are known to be high quality."},{"id":"cont.pdf_cdf","name":"Continuous PDF <-> CDF","unit":"Continuous random variables","priority":"high","cue":"A density is given; integrate to 1, integrate for probabilities, differentiate CDF for PDF.","intuition":"For a continuous variable, probability is area, not height. The CDF accumulates area from the left.","model":"PDF area is probability. F(x)=integral from -infinity to x of f(u). Where differentiable, f(x)=F'(x).","why":"This is the foundation for reasoning about continuous times, lengths, speeds, and other measurements.","trap":"For continuous X, P(X=x)=0 even when f(x) is large. Density height is not point probability.","transfer":"A tall density over a narrow range can still contain less probability than a lower density spread across a wide range."},{"id":"cont.quantiles_transform","name":"Continuous quantiles & transformations","unit":"Continuous random variables","priority":"high","cue":"Solve F(q)=p or derive the distribution of Y=g(X).","intuition":"A quantile is an x-location that captures a chosen amount of cumulative probability. A transformation relabels the x-axis and changes the distribution accordingly.","model":"Quantile q solves F(q)=p. For transformations, derive P(Y<=y) in terms of X, then differentiate if a PDF is needed.","why":"Quantiles help set thresholds and service targets; transformations let you move between units or derived quantities.","trap":"For nonlinear transformations, do not just plug E[X] into g or casually replace x with g(x) in the density.","transfer":"The 90th percentile of response time is the threshold below which 90% of responses finish."},{"id":"dist.uniform","name":"Uniform model","unit":"Continuous distributions","priority":"medium","cue":"Every point in a finite interval has constant density.","intuition":"Uniform means equal density across an interval, so probabilities are just relative lengths.","model":"Uniform(A,B) has density 1/(B-A) on [A,B], mean (A+B)/2, variance (B-A)^2/12.","why":"It is useful when a continuous location or time is modeled as equally likely anywhere inside a bounded window.","trap":"For continuous uniform variables, probabilities come from interval length, not counting endpoints. Individual points have probability zero.","transfer":"If a shuttle arrival is modeled uniformly between 0 and 20 minutes, waiting between 5 and 10 minutes has probability 5/20."},{"id":"dist.exponential","name":"Exponential & memorylessness","unit":"Continuous distributions","priority":"high","cue":"Continuous waiting time with constant rate.","intuition":"Exponential is the waiting-time partner of a constant-rate arrival process. Memorylessness means elapsed waiting does not make the remaining wait shorter or longer.","model":"For X~Exp(lambda), P(X>t)=e^(-lambda t), F(t)=1-e^(-lambda t), mean=1/lambda. It is memoryless.","why":"This is a natural model for time until the next event in simple queueing and reliability settings.","trap":"Lambda is a rate, while 1/lambda is the mean waiting time. Do not swap them.","transfer":"If failures arrive at a constant rate of 0.5 per hour, mean waiting time to the next failure is 2 hours."},{"id":"dist.normal","name":"Normal, z-scores & sums","unit":"Continuous distributions","priority":"high","cue":"Bell-shaped variable or sum/difference of independent normals.","intuition":"The normal is a location-scale family: standardizing turns any normal into the same standard normal shape.","model":"Z=(X-mu)/sigma. Independent normals add: means add and variances add. For X-Y, means subtract while variances still add.","why":"Normal models appear throughout measurement, error, aggregated variation, and statistical inference.","trap":"When subtracting independent normals, variances still add. Only means inherit the minus sign.","transfer":"If two independent task times are normal, their total is normal with means added and variances added."},{"id":"dist.family_recognition","name":"Recognize Beta, Gamma & Weibull","unit":"Continuous distributions","priority":"low","cue":"Identify support or shape, or distinguish these families from Uniform, Exponential, Normal.","intuition":"Start with support before formulas. Beta lives on a bounded 0-to-1 scale; Gamma and Weibull live on positive values and can take many right-skewed shapes.","model":"Beta: support (0,1). Gamma: positive, flexible waiting-time family. Weibull: positive, flexible lifetime/failure family.","why":"Recognition is often enough to rule models in or out quickly before doing any calculation.","trap":"Do not pick a family only because the histogram is right-skewed. Support constraints are often the fastest discriminator.","transfer":"A probability parameter must stay between 0 and 1, making Beta a natural candidate; a positive lifetime can fit Gamma or Weibull."},{"id":"qq.construct","name":"Construct Q-Q plot logic","unit":"Q-Q plots","priority":"high","cue":"Sort data, compute q_i=(i-0.5)/n, compare sample x_(i) with theoretical F^-1(q_i).","intuition":"A Q-Q plot asks whether your ordered sample sits where the proposed distribution says its quantiles should sit.","model":"Pair ordered x_(i) with theoretical F^-1((i-0.5)/n). Plot theoretical quantile on x-axis and sample quantile on y-axis.","why":"It converts an abstract distribution-fit question into a geometric one you can inspect visually.","trap":"Do not forget to sort the observations first, and use the course's q_i=(i-0.5)/n convention.","transfer":"For the 7th smallest observation out of 20, first compute q_7=6.5/20 before finding the theoretical quantile."},{"id":"qq.interpret","name":"Interpret Q-Q plots","unit":"Q-Q plots","priority":"high","cue":"Judge whether a chosen distribution fits.","intuition":"If sample quantiles and theoretical quantiles match, the points trace the reference line. Systematic bends reveal systematic mismatch.","model":"Points close to y=x support the proposed distribution. Random small scatter is fine; sustained curvature is evidence against the fit.","why":"Q-Q plots are a fast diagnostic for whether a probability model is plausible before relying on it.","trap":"A few noisy points are not the same as a systematic pattern. Look for structure in the deviations.","transfer":"If both tails peel away from the line in a consistent way, the issue is not random noise—it says the proposed tail behavior is wrong."}],"problems":[{"id":"data-graphical_summary-1","skill":"data.graphical_summary","stage":"guided","prompt":"A histogram of startup purchase sizes has most purchases between $20 and $80, but a few purchases stretch out to $2,000. What is the shape?","answer":"right-skewed","aliases":["right skewed","right-skewed","right skew","positively skewed"],"hint":"Name the direction of the long tail, not where most of the bars are.","explanation":"The long tail extends toward large values, so the distribution is right-skewed."},{"id":"data-graphical_summary-2","skill":"data.graphical_summary","stage":"independent","prompt":"Two tennis serve-speed distributions have the same center. Player A's boxplot has a much wider box and longer whiskers than Player B's. Who is more variable?","contains":["A"],"hint":"Compare spread, not center.","explanation":"Player A is more variable because the middle 50% and overall non-outlier range are more spread out."},{"id":"data-graphical_summary-3","skill":"data.graphical_summary","stage":"independent","prompt":"A boxplot has Q1=40, median=48, Q3=55, and one point at 120 beyond the upper whisker. What should you call 120?","contains":["outlier"],"hint":"A point plotted separately beyond the whisker is being flagged as unusual.","explanation":"120 is an outlier according to the boxplot rule used to draw the whiskers."},{"id":"data-graphical_summary-4","skill":"data.graphical_summary","stage":"mixed","prompt":"Bug-resolution times cluster around 2-4 hours, with a small number taking 30-60 hours. Would the mean likely be above or below the median?","contains":["above"],"hint":"Long right tails pull one center much more strongly than the other.","explanation":"The mean is likely above the median because large right-tail values pull the mean upward."},{"id":"data-graphical_summary-5","skill":"data.graphical_summary","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: Distribution A is symmetric and narrow. Distribution B is symmetric with the same center but much wider. Which statement is correct: same center, different spread; or different center, same spread?","contains":["same","center","different","spread"],"hint":"Symmetry does not determine width.","explanation":"They have the same center but different spread; B is more variable."},{"id":"data-center_quantiles-1","skill":"data.center_quantiles","stage":"guided","prompt":"Your last five tennis practice rally counts are 8, 10, 11, 13, 38. Find the median.","answer":"11","aliases":["11"],"hint":"Sort first, then take the middle value.","explanation":"The sorted list is already 8,10,11,13,38; the middle value is 11."},{"id":"data-center_quantiles-2","skill":"data.center_quantiles","stage":"independent","prompt":"A small product test gets daily signups 20, 22, 23, 25, 110. What is the mean?","answer":"40","aliases":["40"],"hint":"Add all five values, then divide by 5.","explanation":"(20+22+23+25+110)/5=200/5=40. Notice how the one large day pulls the mean up."},{"id":"data-center_quantiles-3","skill":"data.center_quantiles","stage":"independent","prompt":"For the data 2, 4, 7, 9, 12, what proportion of observations are at or below 7?","answer":"0.6","aliases":[".6","0.6","3/5","60%"],"hint":"Count values at or below 7, then divide by the total.","explanation":"Three of five observations are <=7, so the empirical cumulative proportion is 3/5=0.6."},{"id":"data-center_quantiles-4","skill":"data.center_quantiles","stage":"mixed","prompt":"A startup has user spend values 10, 12, 13, 14, 200. Which is the better description of a typical user, mean or median?","contains":["median"],"hint":"Ask which center resists the extreme value.","explanation":"The median is more representative of a typical user because the $200 value heavily distorts the mean."},{"id":"data-center_quantiles-5","skill":"data.center_quantiles","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: Sorted data are 3, 5, 8, 12, 14, 20, 30. What is the median?","answer":"12","aliases":["12"],"hint":"There are 7 observations, so take the 4th.","explanation":"With 7 ordered observations, the median is the 4th value: 12."},{"id":"data-variance_sd-1","skill":"data.variance_sd","stage":"guided","prompt":"Two players both average 100 mph serves. Player A has SD 3 mph and Player B has SD 9 mph. Who is more consistent?","contains":["A"],"hint":"Smaller standard deviation means values stay closer to the mean.","explanation":"Player A is more consistent because an SD of 3 mph indicates much less spread."},{"id":"data-variance_sd-2","skill":"data.variance_sd","stage":"independent","prompt":"If a dataset has variance 25, what is its standard deviation?","answer":"5","aliases":["5"],"hint":"Standard deviation is the square root of variance.","explanation":"sqrt(25)=5."},{"id":"data-variance_sd-3","skill":"data.variance_sd","stage":"independent","prompt":"If every observation in a dataset is identical, what is the variance?","answer":"0","aliases":["0","zero"],"hint":"How far is each point from the mean?","explanation":"Every deviation from the mean is 0, so the variance is 0."},{"id":"data-variance_sd-4","skill":"data.variance_sd","stage":"mixed","prompt":"System A response times have SD 0.4 s; System B has SD 1.2 s. Their means are equal. Which system is more predictable?","contains":["A"],"hint":"Predictability here means less variation around the same average.","explanation":"System A is more predictable because its standard deviation is smaller."},{"id":"data-variance_sd-5","skill":"data.variance_sd","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: A variable has standard deviation 6. What is its variance?","answer":"36","aliases":["36"],"hint":"Variance is SD squared.","explanation":"6^2=36."},{"id":"data-standardization-1","skill":"data.standardization","stage":"guided","prompt":"You score 90 on an exam with mean 80 and SD 5. What is your z-score?","answer":"2","aliases":["2","2.0"],"hint":"Subtract the mean, then divide by SD.","explanation":"z=(90-80)/5=2, so the score is 2 SD above the mean."},{"id":"data-standardization-2","skill":"data.standardization","stage":"independent","prompt":"A tennis serve is 112 mph in a session with mean 118 and SD 3. What is its z-score?","answer":"-2","aliases":["-2","-2.0"],"hint":"The serve is below the mean, so expect a negative z-score.","explanation":"z=(112-118)/3=-2."},{"id":"data-standardization-3","skill":"data.standardization","stage":"independent","prompt":"A metric has mean 50 and SD 10. What raw value corresponds to z=1.5?","answer":"65","aliases":["65"],"hint":"Rearrange z=(x-mu)/sigma to x=mu+z*sigma.","explanation":"x=50+1.5(10)=65."},{"id":"data-standardization-4","skill":"data.standardization","stage":"mixed","prompt":"Exam A: score 88, mean 80, SD 4. Exam B: score 92, mean 80, SD 8. On which exam is the score more exceptional relative to classmates?","contains":["A"],"hint":"Compare z-scores, not raw scores.","explanation":"Exam A z=2; Exam B z=1.5. The Exam A performance is more exceptional."},{"id":"data-standardization-5","skill":"data.standardization","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: x=70, mean=100, SD=15. Find z.","answer":"-2","aliases":["-2","-2.0"],"hint":"z=(70-100)/15.","explanation":"z=-30/15=-2."},{"id":"count-perm_comb-1","skill":"count.perm_comb","stage":"guided","prompt":"You choose 3 people from 10 to join a project team. Does order matter, and how many teams are possible?","answer":"120","aliases":["120","C(10,3)","10 choose 3"],"hint":"A team has no first, second, third position.","explanation":"Order does not matter, so use C(10,3)=120."},{"id":"count-perm_comb-2","skill":"count.perm_comb","stage":"independent","prompt":"From 8 finalists, you award gold, silver, and bronze. How many outcomes are possible?","answer":"336","aliases":["336","8P3","P(8,3)"],"hint":"Gold/silver/bronze are distinct positions, so order matters.","explanation":"Use permutations: 8*7*6=336."},{"id":"count-perm_comb-3","skill":"count.perm_comb","stage":"independent","prompt":"A product team must choose 4 interviewees from 15 applicants. How many different sets can be chosen?","answer":"1365","aliases":["1365","1,365"],"hint":"Only membership matters.","explanation":"C(15,4)=1365."},{"id":"count-perm_comb-4","skill":"count.perm_comb","stage":"mixed","prompt":"You select a president and vice president from 12 club members. How many assignments are possible?","answer":"132","aliases":["132"],"hint":"Swapping the same two people changes their roles.","explanation":"12 choices for president and 11 for vice president gives 12*11=132."},{"id":"count-perm_comb-5","skill":"count.perm_comb","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: How many 5-person committees can be selected from 14 people?","answer":"2002","aliases":["2002","2,002"],"hint":"Committee membership is unordered.","explanation":"C(14,5)=2002."},{"id":"count-partitions-1","skill":"count.partitions","stage":"guided","prompt":"Six distinct students are split into two unlabeled groups of 3. How many partitions?","answer":"10","aliases":["10"],"hint":"Choose 3, then divide by 2 because swapping the two groups changes nothing.","explanation":"C(6,3)/2=20/2=10."},{"id":"count-partitions-2","skill":"count.partitions","stage":"independent","prompt":"Eight people are split into two unlabeled groups of 4. How many partitions?","answer":"35","aliases":["35"],"hint":"Choose one group of 4 and correct for swapping the groups.","explanation":"C(8,4)/2=70/2=35."},{"id":"count-partitions-3","skill":"count.partitions","stage":"independent","prompt":"Twelve attendees are split into three unlabeled groups of 4. What expression counts the partitions?","contains":["12","4","8","4","4","4","6"],"hint":"Build three groups, then divide by 3! because group labels do not matter.","explanation":"C(12,4)C(8,4)C(4,4)/3! is the standard construction."},{"id":"count-partitions-4","skill":"count.partitions","stage":"mixed","prompt":"Why is C(12,6) too large for splitting 12 people into two unlabeled groups of 6?","contains":["twice"],"hint":"After choosing six, what happens if you instead choose the complementary six first?","explanation":"Each final split is counted twice: once by choosing either side of the same partition as the first group."},{"id":"count-partitions-5","skill":"count.partitions","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: 10 distinct people are split into two unlabeled groups of 5. How many partitions?","answer":"126","aliases":["126"],"hint":"C(10,5)/2.","explanation":"252/2=126."},{"id":"prob-events-1","skill":"prob.events","stage":"guided","prompt":"Let A='at least one serve lands in' over 4 attempts. What is the complement A^c?","contains":["no","serve"],"hint":"'At least one' is negated by 'none.'","explanation":"The complement is 'no serves land in' across all 4 attempts."},{"id":"prob-events-2","skill":"prob.events","stage":"independent","prompt":"If A='user opens the app' and B='user saves a memory', what event is 'opens the app and saves a memory'?","contains":["intersection"],"hint":"'And' means overlap.","explanation":"The event is A intersection B."},{"id":"prob-events-3","skill":"prob.events","stage":"independent","prompt":"If A='bug report has logs' and B='bug report has screenshots', what event is 'has logs or screenshots or both'?","contains":["union"],"hint":"Inclusive 'or' is union.","explanation":"The event is A union B."},{"id":"prob-events-4","skill":"prob.events","stage":"mixed","prompt":"If P(no failures in 5 independent runs)=0.77, what is P(at least one failure)?","answer":"0.23","aliases":[".23","0.23","23%"],"hint":"Use the complement.","explanation":"1-0.77=0.23."},{"id":"prob-events-5","skill":"prob.events","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: A is 'X>3'. Write the complement in words or notation.","contains":["X","3"],"hint":"Not greater than 3 means less than or equal to 3.","explanation":"A^c is {X<=3}."},{"id":"prob-inclusion_exclusion-1","skill":"prob.inclusion_exclusion","stage":"guided","prompt":"P(A)=0.40, P(B)=0.35, P(A intersection B)=0.15. Find P(A union B).","answer":"0.6","aliases":[".6","0.6","60%"],"hint":"Add A and B, then subtract the overlap once.","explanation":"0.40+0.35-0.15=0.60."},{"id":"prob-inclusion_exclusion-2","skill":"prob.inclusion_exclusion","stage":"independent","prompt":"In a survey, 60 users like feature A, 50 like feature B, and 20 like both. How many like at least one?","answer":"90","aliases":["90"],"hint":"The 20 who like both were counted twice in 60+50.","explanation":"60+50-20=90 unique users."},{"id":"prob-inclusion_exclusion-3","skill":"prob.inclusion_exclusion","stage":"independent","prompt":"P(A union B)=0.72, P(A)=0.45, P(B)=0.40. Find P(A intersection B).","answer":"0.13","aliases":[".13","0.13"],"hint":"Rearrange inclusion-exclusion.","explanation":"0.45+0.40-0.72=0.13."},{"id":"prob-inclusion_exclusion-4","skill":"prob.inclusion_exclusion","stage":"mixed","prompt":"A class has 100 students: 55 play tennis, 35 play soccer, 15 play both. What proportion play at least one?","answer":"0.75","aliases":[".75","0.75","75%"],"hint":"Find the union count first.","explanation":"55+35-15=75, so 75/100=0.75."},{"id":"prob-inclusion_exclusion-5","skill":"prob.inclusion_exclusion","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: P(A)=0.2, P(B)=0.5, and A and B are disjoint. Find P(A union B).","answer":"0.7","aliases":[".7","0.7"],"hint":"Disjoint means the overlap is 0.","explanation":"0.2+0.5-0=0.7."},{"id":"prob-equally_likely-1","skill":"prob.equally_likely","stage":"guided","prompt":"A fair six-sided die is rolled. What is P(rolling an even number)?","answer":"0.5","aliases":["1/2",".5","0.5"],"hint":"Count favorable faces over 6 equally likely faces.","explanation":"Even faces are {2,4,6}: 3/6=1/2."},{"id":"prob-equally_likely-2","skill":"prob.equally_likely","stage":"independent","prompt":"One student is selected uniformly from a 20-person roster. Five are on the tennis club. What is P(selecting a tennis-club member)?","answer":"0.25","aliases":["1/4",".25","0.25"],"hint":"Uniform selection makes each student equally likely.","explanation":"5/20=1/4."},{"id":"prob-equally_likely-3","skill":"prob.equally_likely","stage":"independent","prompt":"Two fair coin flips give HH, HT, TH, TT equally likely. What is P(exactly one head)?","answer":"0.5","aliases":["1/2",".5","0.5"],"hint":"Which outcomes have exactly one H?","explanation":"HT and TH are favorable, so 2/4=1/2."},{"id":"prob-equally_likely-4","skill":"prob.equally_likely","stage":"mixed","prompt":"Why is P(rain tomorrow)=1/2 not justified merely because the outcomes are 'rain' and 'no rain'?","contains":["not","equally"],"hint":"Two possible labels do not imply equal chances.","explanation":"The outcomes are not automatically equally likely, so favorable/total counting cannot be used."},{"id":"prob-equally_likely-5","skill":"prob.equally_likely","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: A card is drawn uniformly from a standard 52-card deck. Find P(heart).","answer":"0.25","aliases":["1/4",".25","0.25"],"hint":"There are 13 hearts among 52 equally likely cards.","explanation":"13/52=1/4."},{"id":"cond-conditional-1","skill":"cond.conditional","stage":"guided","prompt":"Among 100 bug reports, 40 include reproduction steps and 30 include both reproduction steps and logs. Given that a report has reproduction steps, what is P(it also has logs)?","answer":"0.75","aliases":[".75","0.75","3/4"],"hint":"Restrict the denominator to the 40 reports with reproduction steps.","explanation":"P(logs | repro)=30/40=0.75."},{"id":"cond-conditional-2","skill":"cond.conditional","stage":"independent","prompt":"A tennis player gets 60 first serves in; 42 of those points are won. Given the first serve landed in, what is P(the point is won)?","answer":"0.7","aliases":[".7","0.7","70%"],"hint":"The conditioning event defines the denominator.","explanation":"42/60=0.70."},{"id":"cond-conditional-3","skill":"cond.conditional","stage":"independent","prompt":"P(A intersection B)=0.18 and P(B)=0.30. Find P(A|B).","answer":"0.6","aliases":[".6","0.6"],"hint":"Divide the overlap by P(B).","explanation":"0.18/0.30=0.60."},{"id":"cond-conditional-4","skill":"cond.conditional","stage":"mixed","prompt":"A product dashboard says 20% of users are Pro and 8% are both Pro and daily active. What is P(daily active | Pro)?","answer":"0.4","aliases":[".4","0.4","40%"],"hint":"The denominator is the Pro population.","explanation":"0.08/0.20=0.40."},{"id":"cond-conditional-5","skill":"cond.conditional","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: P(A)=0.5, P(B)=0.4, P(A intersection B)=0.1. Find P(A|B).","answer":"0.25","aliases":[".25","0.25","1/4"],"hint":"P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B).","explanation":"0.1/0.4=0.25."},{"id":"cond-total_bayes-1","skill":"cond.total_bayes","stage":"guided","prompt":"1% of reports contain a true regression. A detector flags 90% of true regressions and falsely flags 5% of non-regressions. Write the numerator of P(regression | flagged).","contains":["0.9","0.01"],"hint":"Bayes numerator is likelihood times prior.","explanation":"The numerator is P(flagged|regression)P(regression)=0.90*0.01."},{"id":"cond-total_bayes-2","skill":"cond.total_bayes","stage":"independent","prompt":"Two acquisition channels generate users. 70% come from A with 10% conversion; 30% come from B with 20% conversion. What is the overall conversion probability?","answer":"0.13","aliases":[".13","0.13","13%"],"hint":"Weight each channel conversion rate by the channel share.","explanation":"0.7(0.10)+0.3(0.20)=0.07+0.06=0.13."},{"id":"cond-total_bayes-3","skill":"cond.total_bayes","stage":"independent","prompt":"Using the previous setup (A:70% share,10% conversion; B:30% share,20% conversion), given that a user converted, what is P(they came from B)?","answer":"0.461538","aliases":["6/13","0.4615","0.462","46.15%","46.2%"],"hint":"Bayes: B-and-convert divided by total convert.","explanation":"P(B|convert)=0.3(0.2)/0.13=0.06/0.13=6/13≈0.462."},{"id":"cond-total_bayes-4","skill":"cond.total_bayes","stage":"mixed","prompt":"A disease prevalence is 0.001. A test has sensitivity 0.99 and false-positive rate 0.02. Is P(disease | positive) closer to 5%, 50%, or 99%?","contains":["5"],"hint":"The disease is very rare, so false positives from the large healthy group matter.","explanation":"It is about 4.72%, so 5% is closest. High sensitivity does not overcome an extremely low prior by itself."},{"id":"cond-total_bayes-5","skill":"cond.total_bayes","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: P(C1)=0.6, P(C2)=0.4, P(E|C1)=0.2, P(E|C2)=0.5. Find P(C2|E).","answer":"0.625","aliases":[".625","0.625","5/8"],"hint":"First compute P(E)=0.6(0.2)+0.4(0.5).","explanation":"P(E)=0.12+0.20=0.32; P(C2|E)=0.20/0.32=0.625."},{"id":"cond-independence-1","skill":"cond.independence","stage":"guided","prompt":"P(A)=0.5, P(B)=0.4, P(A intersection B)=0.20. Are A and B independent?","contains":["yes"],"hint":"Compare the intersection with P(A)P(B).","explanation":"Yes: 0.5*0.4=0.20."},{"id":"cond-independence-2","skill":"cond.independence","stage":"independent","prompt":"P(A)=0.5, P(B)=0.4, P(A intersection B)=0.10. Are A and B independent?","contains":["no"],"hint":"Check whether 0.10 equals 0.5*0.4.","explanation":"No. Independence would require intersection 0.20."},{"id":"cond-independence-3","skill":"cond.independence","stage":"independent","prompt":"Two cards are drawn without replacement. Is 'first card is an ace' independent of 'second card is an ace'?","contains":["no"],"hint":"Drawing an ace changes how many aces remain.","explanation":"No. The first result changes the probability of an ace on the second draw."},{"id":"cond-independence-4","skill":"cond.independence","stage":"mixed","prompt":"A fair coin is flipped twice. Are 'first flip is heads' and 'second flip is heads' independent?","contains":["yes"],"hint":"The first flip does not change the second flip's probability.","explanation":"Yes. Each has probability 1/2 and the joint probability is 1/4."},{"id":"cond-independence-5","skill":"cond.independence","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: If P(A|B)=P(A)=0.3 and P(B)>0, what does that imply?","contains":["independent"],"hint":"Use the conditional definition of independence.","explanation":"A and B are independent."},{"id":"rv-discrete_pmf_cdf-1","skill":"rv.discrete_pmf_cdf","stage":"guided","prompt":"A discrete CDF jumps from 0.25 just below x=2 to 0.70 at x=2. Find P(X=2).","answer":"0.45","aliases":[".45","0.45"],"hint":"Point mass equals jump size.","explanation":"0.70-0.25=0.45."},{"id":"rv-discrete_pmf_cdf-2","skill":"rv.discrete_pmf_cdf","stage":"independent","prompt":"A PMF has P(X=0)=0.2, P(X=1)=0.5, P(X=2)=0.3. Find F(1).","answer":"0.7","aliases":[".7","0.7"],"hint":"F(1)=P(X<=1).","explanation":"0.2+0.5=0.7."},{"id":"rv-discrete_pmf_cdf-3","skill":"rv.discrete_pmf_cdf","stage":"independent","prompt":"For the same PMF, find P(X>1).","answer":"0.3","aliases":[".3","0.3"],"hint":"Only X=2 is greater than 1.","explanation":"P(X>1)=0.3."},{"id":"rv-discrete_pmf_cdf-4","skill":"rv.discrete_pmf_cdf","stage":"mixed","prompt":"A bug-count CDF satisfies F(3)=0.82. Interpret this in words.","contains":["at most","3"],"hint":"CDF means <=.","explanation":"There is probability 0.82 that the bug count is at most 3."},{"id":"rv-discrete_pmf_cdf-5","skill":"rv.discrete_pmf_cdf","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: F(4)=0.9 and the left limit F(4-)=0.65. Find P(X=4).","answer":"0.25","aliases":[".25","0.25"],"hint":"Subtract the value just before the jump.","explanation":"0.90-0.65=0.25."},{"id":"rv-expectation-1","skill":"rv.expectation","stage":"guided","prompt":"A simple product bet pays $0 with probability 0.7 and $10 with probability 0.3. What is the expected payoff?","answer":"3","aliases":["3","$3"],"hint":"Probability-weight each payoff.","explanation":"E[X]=0(0.7)+10(0.3)=3."},{"id":"rv-expectation-2","skill":"rv.expectation","stage":"independent","prompt":"X takes 1 with probability 0.4 and 3 with probability 0.6. Find E[X].","answer":"2.2","aliases":["2.2"],"hint":"Multiply each value by its probability and add.","explanation":"1(0.4)+3(0.6)=2.2."},{"id":"rv-expectation-3","skill":"rv.expectation","stage":"independent","prompt":"For the same X, find E[X^2].","answer":"5.8","aliases":["5.8"],"hint":"Square each possible X first, then average.","explanation":"1^2(0.4)+3^2(0.6)=0.4+5.4=5.8."},{"id":"rv-expectation-4","skill":"rv.expectation","stage":"mixed","prompt":"A startup outcome is -2 with probability 0.5, 1 with probability 0.4, and 10 with probability 0.1. Find E[X].","answer":"0.4","aliases":[".4","0.4"],"hint":"Expected value can be positive even when a loss is common.","explanation":"-2(0.5)+1(0.4)+10(0.1)=-1+0.4+1=0.4."},{"id":"rv-expectation-5","skill":"rv.expectation","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: X has PMF P(0)=0.25, P(2)=0.50, P(4)=0.25. Find E[X].","answer":"2","aliases":["2","2.0"],"hint":"Weighted average.","explanation":"0(0.25)+2(0.5)+4(0.25)=2."},{"id":"rv-variance_linear-1","skill":"rv.variance_linear","stage":"guided","prompt":"E[X]=10. Let Y=3X+2. Find E[Y].","answer":"32","aliases":["32"],"hint":"Expectation is linear.","explanation":"E[Y]=3E[X]+2=32."},{"id":"rv-variance_linear-2","skill":"rv.variance_linear","stage":"independent","prompt":"Var(X)=4. Let Y=3X+2. Find Var(Y).","answer":"36","aliases":["36"],"hint":"The +2 disappears and 3 is squared.","explanation":"Var(Y)=3^2 Var(X)=9*4=36."},{"id":"rv-variance_linear-3","skill":"rv.variance_linear","stage":"independent","prompt":"SD(X)=5. Let Y=-2X+7. What is SD(Y)?","answer":"10","aliases":["10"],"hint":"Standard deviation scales by |a|.","explanation":"SD(Y)=|-2|*5=10."},{"id":"rv-variance_linear-4","skill":"rv.variance_linear","stage":"mixed","prompt":"You convert minutes X to seconds Y=60X. If Var(X)=2, find Var(Y).","answer":"7200","aliases":["7200","7,200"],"hint":"Variance scales by 60^2.","explanation":"Var(Y)=3600*2=7200."},{"id":"rv-variance_linear-5","skill":"rv.variance_linear","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: Var(X)=9 and Y=X+100. Find Var(Y).","answer":"9","aliases":["9"],"hint":"Adding a constant shifts but does not spread.","explanation":"Var(X+100)=Var(X)=9."},{"id":"dist-binomial-1","skill":"dist.binomial","stage":"guided","prompt":"You hit each first serve independently with probability 0.65. In 10 serves, what distribution models the number that land in?","contains":["bin","10","0.65"],"hint":"Fixed number of independent yes/no trials with constant p.","explanation":"X~Binomial(10,0.65)."},{"id":"dist-binomial-2","skill":"dist.binomial","stage":"independent","prompt":"A landing page converts independently with p=0.2 for each of 8 visitors. What expression gives P(exactly 3 conversions)?","contains":["8","3","0.2","0.8"],"hint":"Choose which 3 convert, then multiply success and failure probabilities.","explanation":"C(8,3)(0.2)^3(0.8)^5."},{"id":"dist-binomial-3","skill":"dist.binomial","stage":"independent","prompt":"X~Bin(20,0.3). Find E[X].","answer":"6","aliases":["6"],"hint":"Binomial mean=np.","explanation":"20*0.3=6."},{"id":"dist-binomial-4","skill":"dist.binomial","stage":"mixed","prompt":"X~Bin(20,0.3). Find Var(X).","answer":"4.2","aliases":["4.2"],"hint":"np(1-p).","explanation":"20(0.3)(0.7)=4.2."},{"id":"dist-binomial-5","skill":"dist.binomial","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: Which condition fails if you draw 10 cards from one deck without replacement and count aces: fixed n, binary outcome, constant p/independence, or none?","contains":["constant","independ"],"hint":"The deck composition changes after each draw.","explanation":"The constant-p/independence condition fails, so exact binomial modeling is not appropriate."},{"id":"dist-poisson-1","skill":"dist.poisson","stage":"guided","prompt":"A repo gets an average of 4 qualifying bug reports per day. Under a Poisson model, what lambda should be used for one day?","answer":"4","aliases":["4","lambda=4"],"hint":"Lambda is the expected count over the modeled interval.","explanation":"For one day, lambda=4."},{"id":"dist-poisson-2","skill":"dist.poisson","stage":"independent","prompt":"The same repo averages 4 reports per day. What lambda should be used for 3 days?","answer":"12","aliases":["12","lambda=12"],"hint":"Scale the rate by the interval length.","explanation":"4 reports/day * 3 days = 12."},{"id":"dist-poisson-3","skill":"dist.poisson","stage":"independent","prompt":"X~Poisson(2). What expression gives P(X=0)?","answer":"e^-2","aliases":["e^(-2)","exp(-2)","e^-2"],"hint":"Poisson PMF is e^-lambda lambda^k/k!.","explanation":"For k=0, P(X=0)=e^-2."},{"id":"dist-poisson-4","skill":"dist.poisson","stage":"mixed","prompt":"A binomial has n=500 and p=0.01. What Poisson lambda gives the usual approximation?","answer":"5","aliases":["5","lambda=5"],"hint":"lambda=np.","explanation":"500(0.01)=5."},{"id":"dist-poisson-5","skill":"dist.poisson","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: If X~Poisson(7), what are E[X] and Var(X)?","contains":["7"],"hint":"For Poisson, mean and variance are both lambda.","explanation":"E[X]=7 and Var(X)=7."},{"id":"dist-geometric-1","skill":"dist.geometric","stage":"guided","prompt":"Each outreach message independently gets a reply with p=0.25. Let X be the message number of the first reply. What distribution is X?","contains":["geometric"],"hint":"Count trials until the first success.","explanation":"X is geometric with p=0.25."},{"id":"dist-geometric-2","skill":"dist.geometric","stage":"independent","prompt":"For p=0.25, what is P(X=3) when X is the trial of the first success?","answer":"0.140625","aliases":[".140625","0.140625","9/64"],"hint":"Fail twice, then succeed.","explanation":"(0.75)^2(0.25)=0.140625."},{"id":"dist-geometric-3","skill":"dist.geometric","stage":"independent","prompt":"A tennis player wins each point independently with p=0.6. What is P(first win occurs on point 1)?","answer":"0.6","aliases":[".6","0.6"],"hint":"No failures precede the first trial.","explanation":"P(X=1)=p=0.6."},{"id":"dist-geometric-4","skill":"dist.geometric","stage":"mixed","prompt":"For a geometric waiting time, you have already failed 5 times. Does that change the success probability on the next independent trial?","contains":["no"],"hint":"Memorylessness.","explanation":"No. With independent constant-p trials, the next-trial success probability is still p."},{"id":"dist-geometric-5","skill":"dist.geometric","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: X~Geom(0.2) counting the successful trial. Write P(X=k).","contains":["0.8","k","0.2"],"hint":"k-1 failures, then one success.","explanation":"P(X=k)=(0.8)^(k-1)(0.2), k=1,2,..."},{"id":"dist-hypergeometric-1","skill":"dist.hypergeometric","stage":"guided","prompt":"A batch has 7 high-quality bug reports and 13 low-quality reports. You sample 5 without replacement. What expression gives P(exactly 2 high-quality)?","contains":["7","2","13","3","20","5"],"hint":"Choose 2 of 7 successes and 3 of 13 failures, divided by all 5-report samples.","explanation":"C(7,2)C(13,3)/C(20,5)."},{"id":"dist-hypergeometric-2","skill":"dist.hypergeometric","stage":"independent","prompt":"A box has 4 red and 6 blue balls; 3 are drawn without replacement. Which model fits the number of red balls?","contains":["hypergeometric"],"hint":"Finite population, two types, without replacement.","explanation":"Use a hypergeometric model."},{"id":"dist-hypergeometric-3","skill":"dist.hypergeometric","stage":"independent","prompt":"Why is the binomial model not exact for drawing cards without replacement?","contains":["depend"],"hint":"Does the first draw change the next probability?","explanation":"The draws are dependent because the composition of the deck changes after each draw."},{"id":"dist-hypergeometric-4","skill":"dist.hypergeometric","stage":"mixed","prompt":"From 12 students, 5 are graduate students. You randomly choose 4 without replacement. What expression gives P(all 4 are graduate students)?","contains":["5","4","12","4"],"hint":"Choose 4 from the 5 graduates over all 4-person samples.","explanation":"C(5,4)C(7,0)/C(12,4)."},{"id":"dist-hypergeometric-5","skill":"dist.hypergeometric","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: Population N=30 contains K=8 successes; sample n=5 without replacement. What distribution models X=# successes?","contains":["hypergeometric"],"hint":"Match the finite-population without-replacement pattern.","explanation":"X is Hypergeometric(N=30,K=8,n=5)."},{"id":"cont-pdf_cdf-1","skill":"cont.pdf_cdf","stage":"guided","prompt":"A density is f(x)=c on 0<=x<=4. Find c.","answer":"0.25","aliases":["1/4",".25","0.25"],"hint":"Total area must equal 1.","explanation":"4c=1, so c=1/4."},{"id":"cont-pdf_cdf-2","skill":"cont.pdf_cdf","stage":"independent","prompt":"For f(x)=1/4 on [0,4], find P(1<=X<=3).","answer":"0.5","aliases":["1/2",".5","0.5"],"hint":"Probability is rectangle area.","explanation":"Width 2 times height 1/4 gives 1/2."},{"id":"cont-pdf_cdf-3","skill":"cont.pdf_cdf","stage":"independent","prompt":"If F(x)=x^2/9 for 0<=x<=3, what is f(x) on that interval?","contains":["2x","9"],"hint":"Differentiate the CDF.","explanation":"f(x)=F'(x)=2x/9."},{"id":"cont-pdf_cdf-4","skill":"cont.pdf_cdf","stage":"mixed","prompt":"For a continuous X, what is P(X=2)?","answer":"0","aliases":["0","zero"],"hint":"Single points have zero area.","explanation":"For a continuous random variable, P(X=2)=0."},{"id":"cont-pdf_cdf-5","skill":"cont.pdf_cdf","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: f(x)=2x for 0<=x<=1. Find P(X<=0.5).","answer":"0.25","aliases":[".25","0.25","1/4"],"hint":"Integrate 2x from 0 to 0.5.","explanation":"Integral_0^.5 2x dx = x^2|_0^.5=0.25."},{"id":"cont-quantiles_transform-1","skill":"cont.quantiles_transform","stage":"guided","prompt":"If F(x)=x^2 on 0<=x<=1, find the median.","answer":"0.7071","aliases":["sqrt(0.5)","1/sqrt(2)","0.707","0.7071"],"hint":"Set F(m)=0.5.","explanation":"m^2=0.5, so m=sqrt(0.5)≈0.7071."},{"id":"cont-quantiles_transform-2","skill":"cont.quantiles_transform","stage":"independent","prompt":"For the same F(x)=x^2 on [0,1], find the 90th percentile.","answer":"0.9487","aliases":["sqrt(0.9)","0.949","0.9487"],"hint":"Set F(q)=0.9.","explanation":"q=sqrt(0.9)≈0.9487."},{"id":"cont-quantiles_transform-3","skill":"cont.quantiles_transform","stage":"independent","prompt":"X is a continuous response time and Y=X+5. If the median of X is 12, what is the median of Y?","answer":"17","aliases":["17"],"hint":"A strictly increasing shift moves every quantile by the same constant.","explanation":"Adding 5 shifts the median from 12 to 17."},{"id":"cont-quantiles_transform-4","skill":"cont.quantiles_transform","stage":"mixed","prompt":"X is positive and Y=2X. If the 75th percentile of X is 8, what is the 75th percentile of Y?","answer":"16","aliases":["16"],"hint":"A positive scaling doubles every quantile.","explanation":"The 75th percentile becomes 2*8=16."},{"id":"cont-quantiles_transform-5","skill":"cont.quantiles_transform","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: A quantile q satisfies F(q)=0.25. What quantile is q?","contains":["25"],"hint":"Translate cumulative probability into percentile language.","explanation":"q is the 25th percentile, also called the first quartile."},{"id":"dist-uniform-1","skill":"dist.uniform","stage":"guided","prompt":"A shuttle arrival time is Uniform(0,20) minutes. Find P(wait<=5).","answer":"0.25","aliases":[".25","0.25","1/4"],"hint":"Uniform probability is favorable interval length / total length.","explanation":"5/20=0.25."},{"id":"dist-uniform-2","skill":"dist.uniform","stage":"independent","prompt":"X~Uniform(10,30). Find E[X].","answer":"20","aliases":["20"],"hint":"Mean is the midpoint.","explanation":"(10+30)/2=20."},{"id":"dist-uniform-3","skill":"dist.uniform","stage":"independent","prompt":"X~Uniform(0,12). Find Var(X).","answer":"12","aliases":["12"],"hint":"Variance=(B-A)^2/12.","explanation":"12^2/12=12."},{"id":"dist-uniform-4","skill":"dist.uniform","stage":"mixed","prompt":"A meeting starts uniformly sometime between 2:00 and 2:30. What is P(it starts between 2:10 and 2:20)?","answer":"0.333333","aliases":["1/3",".333","0.333","33.3%"],"hint":"Compare the 10-minute favorable window to the 30-minute total window.","explanation":"10/30=1/3."},{"id":"dist-uniform-5","skill":"dist.uniform","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: X~Uniform(-2,6). What is the density height?","answer":"0.125","aliases":["1/8",".125","0.125"],"hint":"Height=1/(B-A).","explanation":"1/(6-(-2))=1/8."},{"id":"dist-exponential-1","skill":"dist.exponential","stage":"guided","prompt":"A system failure time is Exp(lambda=0.5 per hour). What is the mean waiting time?","answer":"2","aliases":["2","2 hours"],"hint":"Mean=1/lambda.","explanation":"1/0.5=2 hours."},{"id":"dist-exponential-2","skill":"dist.exponential","stage":"independent","prompt":"X~Exp(0.25). Write P(X>4).","answer":"e^-1","aliases":["e^(-1)","exp(-1)","e^-1"],"hint":"Survival probability is e^(-lambda t).","explanation":"e^(-0.25*4)=e^-1."},{"id":"dist-exponential-3","skill":"dist.exponential","stage":"independent","prompt":"X~Exp(lambda). Given X>10, what is the distribution of the additional waiting time X-10?","contains":["exponential","lambda"],"hint":"Use memorylessness.","explanation":"The remaining waiting time is again Exp(lambda)."},{"id":"dist-exponential-4","skill":"dist.exponential","stage":"mixed","prompt":"Bug reports arrive so that waiting time is Exp(2 per hour). What is P(wait>1 hour)?","answer":"e^-2","aliases":["e^(-2)","exp(-2)","e^-2"],"hint":"P(X>t)=e^(-lambda t).","explanation":"e^(-2*1)=e^-2."},{"id":"dist-exponential-5","skill":"dist.exponential","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: X~Exp(4). Find F(0.5).","answer":"1-e^-2","aliases":["1-e^(-2)","1-exp(-2)","1-e^-2"],"hint":"F(t)=1-e^(-lambda t).","explanation":"F(0.5)=1-e^(-4*0.5)=1-e^-2."},{"id":"dist-normal-1","skill":"dist.normal","stage":"guided","prompt":"X~N(100,15^2). What z-score corresponds to X=130?","answer":"2","aliases":["2","2.0"],"hint":"(130-100)/15.","explanation":"z=2."},{"id":"dist-normal-2","skill":"dist.normal","stage":"independent","prompt":"X1~N(10,4) and X2~N(20,9) are independent, where the second parameter is variance. What is the distribution of X1+X2?","contains":["30","13"],"hint":"Add means and independent variances.","explanation":"X1+X2~N(30,13)."},{"id":"dist-normal-3","skill":"dist.normal","stage":"independent","prompt":"X1~N(10,4) and X2~N(20,9) are independent. What is the distribution of X1-X2?","contains":["-10","13"],"hint":"Means subtract; variances still add.","explanation":"X1-X2~N(-10,13)."},{"id":"dist-normal-4","skill":"dist.normal","stage":"mixed","prompt":"Two independent task times are N(30,25) minutes each. What are the mean and variance of their total?","contains":["60","50"],"hint":"Add both means and both variances.","explanation":"Total mean=60 and variance=50."},{"id":"dist-normal-5","skill":"dist.normal","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: X~N(mu,sigma^2). What transformation gives a standard normal Z?","contains":["X","mu","sigma"],"hint":"Center then divide by standard deviation.","explanation":"Z=(X-mu)/sigma."},{"id":"dist-family_recognition-1","skill":"dist.family_recognition","stage":"guided","prompt":"You model a probability parameter that must stay between 0 and 1. Which family among Beta, Gamma, and Weibull is immediately compatible with that support?","contains":["Beta"],"hint":"Start with support.","explanation":"Beta is supported on (0,1), making it the natural candidate."},{"id":"dist-family_recognition-2","skill":"dist.family_recognition","stage":"independent","prompt":"A variable is a positive waiting time with a flexible right-skewed shape. Which two families among Beta, Gamma, Weibull remain plausible by support alone?","contains":["Gamma","Weibull"],"hint":"Both live on positive values.","explanation":"Gamma and Weibull both have positive support."},{"id":"dist-family_recognition-3","skill":"dist.family_recognition","stage":"independent","prompt":"A variable can take negative and positive values. Can a standard Beta, Gamma, or Weibull distribution directly model it?","contains":["no"],"hint":"Check each family's support.","explanation":"No. Beta is bounded in (0,1), while Gamma and Weibull are positive."},{"id":"dist-family_recognition-4","skill":"dist.family_recognition","stage":"mixed","prompt":"Which family is most naturally associated with a random proportion such as a user's latent conversion probability: Beta, Gamma, or Weibull?","contains":["Beta"],"hint":"A proportion lies between 0 and 1.","explanation":"Beta is the support-matched family."},{"id":"dist-family_recognition-5","skill":"dist.family_recognition","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: Which of Beta, Gamma, Weibull has bounded support on (0,1)?","contains":["Beta"],"hint":"This is the fastest recognition fact.","explanation":"Beta."},{"id":"qq-construct-1","skill":"qq.construct","stage":"guided","prompt":"For n=10 sorted observations, what q_i is paired with x_(1) using q_i=(i-0.5)/n?","answer":"0.05","aliases":[".05","0.05","0.5/10"],"hint":"Plug i=1 and n=10 into the formula.","explanation":"q_1=(1-0.5)/10=0.05."},{"id":"qq-construct-2","skill":"qq.construct","stage":"independent","prompt":"For n=10, what q_i is paired with x_(10)?","answer":"0.95","aliases":[".95","0.95","9.5/10"],"hint":"Use i=10.","explanation":"q_10=9.5/10=0.95."},{"id":"qq-construct-3","skill":"qq.construct","stage":"independent","prompt":"For n=20, find q_7.","answer":"0.325","aliases":[".325","0.325","6.5/20"],"hint":"(7-0.5)/20.","explanation":"6.5/20=0.325."},{"id":"qq-construct-4","skill":"qq.construct","stage":"mixed","prompt":"When constructing the course's Q-Q plot, what goes on the x-axis: theoretical quantiles or sample observations?","contains":["theoretical"],"hint":"The current course convention compares theoretical x to sample y.","explanation":"Theoretical quantiles go on the x-axis and ordered sample observations on the y-axis."},{"id":"qq-construct-5","skill":"qq.construct","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: What must you do to the sample before pairing observations with q_i values?","contains":["sort"],"hint":"Q-Q plots use order statistics x_(i).","explanation":"Sort the sample from smallest to largest."},{"id":"qq-interpret-1","skill":"qq.interpret","stage":"guided","prompt":"A normal Q-Q plot lies close to y=x with only small unsystematic scatter. What conclusion is supported?","contains":["good","fit"],"hint":"Look for systematic departure, not tiny noise.","explanation":"The normal model is a plausible/good fit."},{"id":"qq-interpret-2","skill":"qq.interpret","stage":"independent","prompt":"A Q-Q plot shows strong smooth curvature away from the reference line. What does that suggest?","contains":["not","fit"],"hint":"Systematic curvature is evidence of mismatch.","explanation":"The proposed distribution is not a good fit."},{"id":"qq-interpret-3","skill":"qq.interpret","stage":"independent","prompt":"Three points deviate slightly but the rest track the line without a pattern. Is that alone strong evidence against the model?","contains":["no"],"hint":"Random small scatter is expected.","explanation":"No. A few small deviations are weaker evidence than systematic curvature."},{"id":"qq-interpret-4","skill":"qq.interpret","stage":"mixed","prompt":"Why is a Q-Q plot more informative than checking only whether the histogram looks roughly bell-shaped?","contains":["quantile"],"hint":"It compares the full ordered distribution against theoretical positions.","explanation":"A Q-Q plot compares sample quantiles to theoretical quantiles across the distribution, making systematic mismatches easier to see."},{"id":"qq-interpret-5","skill":"qq.interpret","stage":"timed","prompt":"Exam-style: If points increasingly bend away from y=x in the tails, should you trust the proposed tail behavior?","contains":["no"],"hint":"Tail departures mean theoretical and observed extreme quantiles disagree.","explanation":"No. The systematic tail deviation indicates the proposed distribution misses the observed tail behavior."}]};
